  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-2 mb-4">Why this segment buys differently</h2>
  <p>
    A toy retailer buys what sells. An educational distributor buys what can be justified — to a purchasing officer, to a
    teacher, sometimes to a parent committee. That shifts the whole conversation from "what does it do" to "what is it
    made of, what age is it for, and what happens if a child puts it in their mouth".
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence is that a supplier who can answer those three questions in writing wins the account even at
    a higher price. Part of our STEM range exists for exactly that reason: soft TPE rather than hard plastic, a documented
    material, and an age grade that matches how the product is actually used in an early-years room.
  </p>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">The three questions that decide the order</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What is it made of?</strong> "Non-toxic plastic" is not an answer. TPE, ABS, PP and silicone behave
      differently, age differently and are regulated differently. Ask for the material by name.</li>
    <li><strong>What age is it graded for, and why?</strong> The grade follows the small-parts assessment and the
      intended use. A soft block set can be graded for toddlers; a gel kit cannot.</li>
    <li><strong>What happens when it is misused?</strong> This is the question a school risk assessment asks. A soft,
      flexible block deforms instead of shattering; a small moulded part can be a choking hazard regardless of the
      material.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-navy mt-10 mb-4">Selling to institutions, not to shoppers</h2>
  <p>
    Institutional buyers order in predictable cycles — the school year, the budget year, the grant cycle — and they
    reorder the same item rather than experimenting. That favours a small, stable range over a wide catalogue, and it
    favours a supplier who can still make the same item in three years' time. When you are choosing between two similar
    STEM items, ask which mould is likely to still be in production, not which one is cheaper today.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also means documentation has to be repeatable. A test report that exists for one production run is not much use if
    the item is reordered annually and the buyer needs a current file every year. Tell us if the account requires an
    annual refresh and we will factor it into the quotation rather than surprise you at renewal.
  </p>

